But when you take all the gear and lay it out

258
00:13:04,400 --> 00:13:06,400
and have them pick off the pack and say,

259
00:13:06,400 --> 00:13:08,400
all right, like carry this around the block,

260
00:13:08,400 --> 00:13:10,400
I think we started doing that where we just

261
00:13:10,400 --> 00:13:12,400
like brought 30 pounds of canned food and put

262
00:13:12,400 --> 00:13:14,400
it in a backpack and we're like this is what

263
00:13:14,400 --> 00:13:16,400
it's going to feel like

264
00:13:16,400 --> 00:13:20,400
That was a more efficient descriptor than

265
00:13:20,400 --> 00:13:23,400
anything else. or overloading a pack and putting

266
00:13:23,400 --> 00:13:25,400
it on somebody and being like this is what

267
00:13:25,400 --> 00:13:27,400
it feels like after 10 miles.

268
00:13:27,400 --> 00:13:29,400
But you did that with some guys who still

269
00:13:29,400 --> 00:13:30,400
didn't understand.

270
00:13:30,400 --> 00:13:31,400
That's true.

271
00:13:31,400 --> 00:13:32,400
Yeah,

272
00:13:32,400 --> 00:13:33,400
I don't know.

273
00:13:33,400 --> 00:13:36,400
I think it's very, very tough to describe

274
00:13:36,400 --> 00:13:38,400
how difficult it really is.

275
00:13:38,400 --> 00:13:39,400
Yeah,

276
00:13:39,400 --> 00:13:43,400
Until you're crying at the end of the day.

277
00:13:43,400 --> 00:13:44,400
Yeah,

278
00:13:44,400 --> 00:13:46,400
But here's my point:

279
00:13:46,400 --> 00:13:48,400
You can't really understand what you're

280
00:13:48,400 --> 00:13:51,400
doing in a lot of stuff that scouts do

281
00:13:51,400 --> 00:13:54,400
until you actually do it and then you learn

282
00:13:54,400 --> 00:13:56,400
how to do it and it gets better,

283
00:13:56,400 --> 00:13:57,400
Absolutely.

284
00:13:57,400 --> 00:13:58,400
Yeah,

285
00:13:58,400 --> 00:13:59,400
Yeah,

286
00:13:59,400 --> 00:14:00,400
That is very true.

287
00:14:00,400 --> 00:14:03,400
Before my first trip you could have told me

288
00:14:03,400 --> 00:14:06,400
how great hiking boots are. all day long

289
00:14:06,400 --> 00:14:08,400
I would have still shown up in sneakers

290
00:14:08,400 --> 00:14:11,400
And I'm glad I learned that, because now

291
00:14:12,400 --> 00:14:14,400
I know the value of good footwear,

292
00:14:14,400 --> 00:14:16,400
And that's one of those things you learn

293
00:14:16,400 --> 00:14:20,400
on trips like that, because it's better

294
00:14:20,400 --> 00:14:21,400
than learning it alone.

295
00:14:21,400 --> 00:14:23,400
At least you've got other people there who've

296
00:14:23,400 --> 00:14:25,400
done this several times and like, yeah,

297
00:14:25,400 --> 00:14:26,400
all right, buddy,

298
00:14:26,400 --> 00:14:28,400
This is how you set up camps,

299
00:14:28,400 --> 00:14:29,400
This is how you start a fire,

300
00:14:29,400 --> 00:14:31,400
Or this is how you go hiking like this.

301
00:14:31,400 --> 00:14:34,400
It's those experiences on the trips

302
00:14:34,400 --> 00:14:37,400
that will help you, I guess. learn

303
00:14:37,400 --> 00:14:39,400
the lessons, learn how to do everything

304
00:14:39,400 --> 00:14:40,400
you need to do.

305
00:14:40,400 --> 00:14:43,400
I think that's a big part of what

306
00:14:43,400 --> 00:14:45,400
scouting does- to make people better

307
00:14:45,400 --> 00:14:48,400
People- is you get humbled constantly

308
00:14:48,400 --> 00:14:51,400
at that age where you need to be humbled,

309
00:14:51,400 --> 00:14:53,400
Where you're like: I know everything better

310
00:14:53,400 --> 00:14:55,400
than all of these people telling me what I should do,

311
00:14:55,400 --> 00:14:57,400
And then you do it wrong and you're like,

312
00:14:57,400 --> 00:14:59,400
oh, I should have done it that way.

313
00:14:59,400 --> 00:15:03,400
And it helps you listen in a very harsh way:

314
00:15:03,400 --> 00:15:04,400
Yeah,

315
00:15:04,400 --> 00:15:05,400
Yeah,

316
00:15:05,400 --> 00:15:06,400
Dang that guy,

317
00:15:06,400 --> 00:15:07,400
I hate him, but he's right.

318
00:15:07,400 --> 00:15:08,400
Yeah,

319
00:15:08,400 --> 00:15:09,400
Yeah.

320
00:15:09,400 --> 00:15:12,400
And it helps you be more accepting of that,

321
00:15:12,400 --> 00:15:14,400
when somebody tells you something, to actually

322
00:15:14,400 --> 00:15:16,400
think about it instead of be like,

323
00:15:16,400 --> 00:15:19,400
no, I'm going to do it my way because it's my way.

324
00:15:19,400 --> 00:15:21,400
Yeah, just like 75 pounds of MREs,

325
00:15:21,400 --> 00:15:22,400
Why not?

326
00:15:22,400 --> 00:15:24,400
Yeah, exactly,

327
00:15:24,400 --> 00:15:25,400
Exactly.

328
00:15:25,400 --> 00:15:28,400
There's all kinds of platitudes about through hiking

329
00:15:28,400 --> 00:15:31,400
and everything about everybody hikes their own hike.

330
00:15:31,400 --> 00:15:33,400
I think it happens in scouts-

331
00:15:33,400 --> 00:15:35,400
maybe in a smaller context,

332
00:15:35,400 --> 00:15:38,400
but I don't know. when you're 11 or 12 or 13 years old,

333
00:15:38,400 --> 00:15:40,400
it's a pretty big context for you.

334
00:15:40,400 --> 00:15:43,400
I think it's just as sizable in scouts

335
00:15:43,400 --> 00:15:45,400
as it is now hitting it.

336
00:15:45,400 --> 00:15:49,400
I mean, we're 52.4 miles in or something like that,

337
00:15:49,400 --> 00:15:51,400
And we've been hit hard.

338
00:15:51,400 --> 00:15:53,400
It's not necessarily the same lesson

339
00:15:53,400 --> 00:15:55,400
because we were better prepared than we were

340
00:15:55,400 --> 00:15:56,400
when we were scouts,

341
00:15:56,400 --> 00:15:58,400
but it's the same sort of message.

342
00:15:58,400 --> 00:16:01,400
You both went on to become eagle scouts

343
00:16:01,400 --> 00:16:02,400
in spite of everything.

344
00:16:02,400 --> 00:16:03,400
Yeah,

345
00:16:03,400 --> 00:16:06,400
It's amazing how that happened.

346
00:16:06,400 --> 00:16:08,400
So how long ago was that?

347
00:16:08,400 --> 00:16:11,400
For me, my paperwork was submitted

348
00:16:11,400 --> 00:16:13,400
just before the deadline,

349
00:16:13,400 --> 00:16:16,400
So that was early 2010..

350
00:16:16,400 --> 00:16:18,400
That was, yeah, seven years ago,

351
00:16:18,400 --> 00:16:20,400
2008 for me,

352
00:16:20,400 --> 00:16:22,400
And you were a little less of a deadline guy.

353
00:16:22,400 --> 00:16:25,400
I think I was maybe a month ahead

354
00:16:25,400 --> 00:16:28,400
of being on the deadline.

355
00:16:28,400 --> 00:16:31,400
Yeah, I was paperwork submitted

356
00:16:31,400 --> 00:16:33,400
just before my 18th birthday.

357
00:16:33,400 --> 00:16:34,400
Yeah,

358
00:16:34,400 --> 00:16:35,400
I do remember that way

359
00:16:35,400 --> 00:16:36,400
because you were one of the guys

360
00:16:36,400 --> 00:16:38,400
where we were counting hours.

361
00:16:38,400 --> 00:16:40,400
Yeah,

362
00:16:40,400 --> 00:16:42,400
It was pretty close, but I made it.

363
00:16:42,400 --> 00:16:47,400
It's been either seven or nine years, right,

364
00:16:47,400 --> 00:16:49,400
So worth doing.

365
00:16:49,400 --> 00:16:50,400
Absolutely.

366
00:16:50,400 --> 00:16:52,400
Yeah, absolutely,

367
00:16:52,400 --> 00:16:56,400
In the years since becoming an eagle scout.

368
00:16:56,400 --> 00:16:58,400
I mean, I've still been involved in scouts.

369
00:16:58,400 --> 00:17:00,400
I think last year is one of the first years

370
00:17:00,400 --> 00:17:02,400
where I really wasn't involved.

371
00:17:02,400 --> 00:17:04,400
One of the great things that happened in the process

372
00:17:04,400 --> 00:17:05,400
of becoming an eagle scout

373
00:17:05,400 --> 00:17:07,400
was learning how to work with other people

374
00:17:07,400 --> 00:17:09,400
and how to manage a project like that

375
00:17:09,400 --> 00:17:11,400
and how to manage other people more so

376
00:17:11,400 --> 00:17:13,400
than just being a patrol leader,

377
00:17:13,400 --> 00:17:14,400
seeing a patrol leader

378
00:17:14,400 --> 00:17:17,400
actually having a significant task at hand

379
00:17:17,400 --> 00:17:18,400
and being able to accomplish it,

380
00:17:18,400 --> 00:17:21,400
because that transferred into working at Camp Horseshoe,

381
00:17:21,400 --> 00:17:23,400
that transferred into being trained

382
00:17:23,400 --> 00:17:24,400
at National Camp School

383
00:17:24,400 --> 00:17:26,400
and then working at National Camp School.

384
00:17:26,400 --> 00:17:28,400
There were so many skills that I learned there

385
00:17:28,400 --> 00:17:29,400
that really helped out

386
00:17:29,400 --> 00:17:31,400
And those were things that I started learning

387
00:17:31,400 --> 00:17:33,400
with the eagle project.

388
00:17:33,400 --> 00:17:35,400
So I definitely really enjoyed doing it.

389
00:17:35,400 --> 00:17:37,400
What translates for you, Bucky?

390
00:17:37,400 --> 00:17:41,400
I think a large part of my person comes from that task.

391
00:17:41,400 --> 00:17:43,400
I think that's probably to this point.

392
00:17:43,400 --> 00:17:46,400
one of my greatest accomplishments would be getting eagle,

393
00:17:46,400 --> 00:17:49,400
And it was kind of alarming that I did

394
00:17:49,400 --> 00:17:52,400
because I hit first class and just sat there

395
00:17:52,400 --> 00:17:54,400
And I was like I don't care.

396
00:17:54,400 --> 00:17:56,400
And then I think when I say around 16,

397
00:17:56,400 --> 00:17:58,400
I was like, well, I'm going to do this.

398
00:17:58,400 --> 00:18:00,400
So I had to scramble again,

399
00:18:00,400 --> 00:18:03,400
But the takeaway is definitely communication skills,

400
00:18:03,400 --> 00:18:04,400
leadership skills.

401
00:18:04,400 --> 00:18:07,400
It's one of those things that sounds kind of cheesy to say

402
00:18:07,400 --> 00:18:08,400
I'd be like be in a scout

403
00:18:08,400 --> 00:18:09,400
taught me how to be a leader,

404
00:18:09,400 --> 00:18:10,400
But it really did.

405
00:18:10,400 --> 00:18:12,400
And it also taught me when other people take charge,

406
00:18:12,400 --> 00:18:14,400
because I have a problem with that.

407
00:18:14,400 --> 00:18:18,400
After I got a taste of control as a patrol leader-

408
00:18:18,400 --> 00:18:20,400
I loved it way too much,

409
00:18:20,400 --> 00:18:23,400
But you learned to take a backseat after,

410
00:18:23,400 --> 00:18:25,400
Because I was. I think I was surprised

411
00:18:25,400 --> 00:18:27,400
after I was senior patrol leader

412
00:18:27,400 --> 00:18:31,400
And I maybe did something else after senior patrol leader.

413
00:18:31,400 --> 00:18:34,400
So I had to go back to being answering to somebody else

414
00:18:34,400 --> 00:18:36,400
and letting them fill that role

415
00:18:36,400 --> 00:18:38,400
And I think that was almost just as important

416
00:18:38,400 --> 00:18:41,400
as learning to be a leader is when to be a leader.

417
00:18:41,400 --> 00:18:42,400
That's pretty interesting.

418
00:18:42,400 --> 00:18:43,400
I like that.

419
00:18:43,400 --> 00:18:44,400
That's a good thought.

420
00:18:44,400 --> 00:18:47,400
Like when you understand how to lead things,

421
00:18:47,400 --> 00:18:50,400
you also understand more about how to be a decent follower.

422
00:18:51,400 --> 00:18:54,400
I think that's something that we picked up from someone named

423
00:18:54,400 --> 00:18:58,400
Clark: letting us make our own mistakes.

424
00:18:58,400 --> 00:19:01,400
It was invaluable.

425
00:19:01,400 --> 00:19:04,400
There's a big learning in making the mistakes yourself

426
00:19:04,400 --> 00:19:08,400
and then having someone there that can laugh a little

427
00:19:08,400 --> 00:19:11,400
and say I told you so, but also we know that it's there

428
00:19:11,400 --> 00:19:14,400
to help us if we really need it.

429
00:19:14,400 --> 00:19:19,400
So we weren't incredibly cruel, just kind of normally cruel.

430
00:19:20,400 --> 00:19:21,400
No, no, no, no,

431
00:19:21,400 --> 00:19:26,400
You're cruel at just the right time-

432
00:19:26,400 --> 00:19:31,400
Surgical cruelness, you know.

433
00:19:31,400 --> 00:19:34,400
So what do your folks think of all this?

434
00:19:34,400 --> 00:19:35,400
My parents are very excited.

435
00:19:35,400 --> 00:19:38,400
My mom has wanted me to hike the Appalachian Trail

436
00:19:38,400 --> 00:19:40,400
for a very long time

437
00:19:40,400 --> 00:19:44,400
And I don't want to say I never really wanted to,

438
00:19:44,400 --> 00:19:46,400
but I never really thought I would-

439
00:19:46,400 --> 00:19:48,400
And she had friends who would go and hike

440
00:19:48,400 --> 00:19:51,400
and do all these sorts of things.

441
00:19:51,400 --> 00:19:53,400
And she would always get very excited and like, wait,

442
00:19:53,400 --> 00:19:54,400
what are you going to do this?

443
00:19:54,400 --> 00:19:56,400
And I never really thought about doing it.

444
00:19:56,400 --> 00:19:59,400
So when the idea came up, when Bucky was first pitching me

445
00:19:59,400 --> 00:20:02,400
the idea of, like I'm doing this, are you coming with me?

446
00:20:02,400 --> 00:20:04,400
And I was sort of sold right then

447
00:20:04,400 --> 00:20:07,400
I first told my mom because my dad would have freaked out

448
00:20:07,400 --> 00:20:09,400
and been like: no, you can't run away into the woods

449
00:20:09,400 --> 00:20:10,400
for a couple of months,

450
00:20:10,400 --> 00:20:12,400
Because that's what I do all summer already.

451
00:20:12,400 --> 00:20:15,400
So I went to my mom's first and was like, mom,

452
00:20:15,400 --> 00:20:17,400
I think I'm going to hike the trail with Bucky.

453
00:20:17,400 --> 00:20:19,400
And she was like, she was so excited.

454
00:20:19,400 --> 00:20:21,400
She was in the first moment

455
00:20:21,400 --> 00:20:22,400
She was like: yes, absolutely,

456
00:20:22,400 --> 00:20:23,400
That's great,

457
00:20:23,400 --> 00:20:24,400
I'll get you a guidebook,

458
00:20:24,400 --> 00:20:25,400
I'll get in touch with my friends along the trail,

459
00:20:25,400 --> 00:20:26,400
It'll be great,

460
00:20:26,400 --> 00:20:27,400
It'll be awesome.

461
00:20:27,400 --> 00:20:29,400
And then I got in touch with my dad and was like: hey, dad,

462
00:20:29,400 --> 00:20:32,400
by the way, this spring this

463
00:20:32,400 --> 00:20:34,400
I'm going to be hiking the Appalachian Trail.

464
00:20:34,400 --> 00:20:37,400
And he was all right with it.

465
00:20:37,400 --> 00:20:39,400
I think he's very excited now that I'm actually here

466
00:20:39,400 --> 00:20:40,400
and doing it.

467
00:20:40,400 --> 00:20:43,400
But I'm sure they were worried leading up to it

468
00:20:43,400 --> 00:20:48,400
that I wasn't prepared or was going to run into bad weather

469
00:20:48,400 --> 00:20:49,400
or whatever.

470
00:20:49,400 --> 00:20:52,400
But now that I'm out here, now that I'm doing it,

471
00:20:52,400 --> 00:20:55,400
I think they're just very excited that I'm in the woods

472
00:20:55,400 --> 00:20:57,400
and using my skills.

473
00:20:57,400 --> 00:20:58,400
They're so supportive.

474
00:20:58,400 --> 00:20:59,400
That's good.

475
00:20:59,400 --> 00:21:00,400
Yeah, supportive.

476
00:21:00,400 --> 00:21:02,400
Bucky, you found the same thing.

477
00:21:02,400 --> 00:21:03,400
Yeah,

478
00:21:03,400 --> 00:21:05,400
It's a combination of pride and jealousy for my mom.

479
00:21:05,400 --> 00:21:07,400
Mom's been doing the long trail

480
00:21:07,400 --> 00:21:11,400
So she's got better and more gear than me.

481
00:21:11,400 --> 00:21:13,400
So she was very supportive.

482
00:21:13,400 --> 00:21:14,400
She knows it's my thing,

483
00:21:14,400 --> 00:21:16,400
So, and Joe's- Joe's super proud.

484
00:21:16,400 --> 00:21:18,400
He sent me a text about it.

485
00:21:18,400 --> 00:21:22,400
It was a little heartbreaking.

486
00:21:22,400 --> 00:21:23,400
There's what

487
00:21:23,400 --> 00:21:27,400
Probably a thousand people who give it any serious thought

488
00:21:27,400 --> 00:21:29,400
at all, for every person that actually makes it

489
00:21:29,400 --> 00:21:31,400
to Springer Mountain.

490
00:21:31,400 --> 00:21:32,400
Yeah, absolutely,

491
00:21:32,400 --> 00:21:34,400
You guys started kind of early, didn't you?

492
00:21:34,400 --> 00:21:37,400
We got to the like visitor center at Amacola Falls,

493
00:21:37,400 --> 00:21:40,400
which is where a lot of through hikers originally register,

494
00:21:40,400 --> 00:21:44,400
And that was February 6th, I think this past Monday.

495
00:21:44,400 --> 00:21:47,400
So when we signed in at the register we were numbers 39

496
00:21:47,400 --> 00:21:49,400
and 40 that had registered.

497
00:21:49,400 --> 00:21:50,400
So we are on the early end.

498
00:21:50,400 --> 00:21:53,400
I've got a good friend from Corsu Milton.

499
00:21:53,400 --> 00:21:56,400
He started January 14th I think was his first day-

500
00:21:56,400 --> 00:21:57,400
Oh no kidding.

501
00:21:57,400 --> 00:21:59,400
And he just got out of the Smokies.

502
00:21:59,400 --> 00:22:02,400
Getting out of the Smokies alive is an accomplishment,

503
00:22:02,400 --> 00:22:04,400
Yeah, especially this early.

504
00:22:04,400 --> 00:22:07,400
He had a rough go at it, but he's out and still kicking,

505
00:22:07,400 --> 00:22:08,400
So that's good.

506
00:22:08,400 --> 00:22:12,400
The trails kind of front loaded with a lot of really stiff hiking,

507
00:22:12,400 --> 00:22:13,400
isn't it?

508
00:22:13,400 --> 00:22:15,400
We thought Georgia would be a breeze

509
00:22:15,400 --> 00:22:19,400
and we have hit a lot of difficulty.

510
00:22:19,400 --> 00:22:22,400
Yeah, there's a switchback or two, I understand,

511
00:22:22,400 --> 00:22:24,400
You know. yeah,

512
00:22:24,400 --> 00:22:27,400
Some like spring loaded rocks.

513
00:22:27,400 --> 00:22:28,400
Yeah,

514
00:22:28,400 --> 00:22:33,400
Do you have a date in mind when you would like to be on Katahdin?

515
00:22:33,400 --> 00:22:36,400
I have a job in the Adirondacks as a summit steward

516
00:22:36,400 --> 00:22:37,400
again this summer,

517
00:22:37,400 --> 00:22:40,400
So that starts Memorial Day weekend.

518
00:22:40,400 --> 00:22:43,400
So I'm hoping I can get to Vermont,

519
00:22:43,400 --> 00:22:45,400
maybe even New Hampshire, by then,

520
00:22:45,400 --> 00:22:49,400
Even if we had done our super outrageous plan of 20 miles a day

521
00:22:49,400 --> 00:22:50,400
from the start.

522
00:22:50,400 --> 00:22:54,400
we wouldn't have gotten to Katahdin until early June.

523
00:22:54,400 --> 00:22:59,400
So I don't think I'll be making it until I take some time off

524
00:22:59,400 --> 00:23:01,400
of being a summit steward,

525
00:23:01,400 --> 00:23:03,400
But hopefully I do at some point this summer.

526
00:23:03,400 --> 00:23:05,400
I'd like to be done before July,

527
00:23:05,400 --> 00:23:07,400
but I have no schedule.

